{"window_id": "2023-12-05#1", "date": "2023-12-05", "window_tokens": 3000, "examples_by_attribute": {}}
{"window_id": "2023-12-05#0", "date": "2023-12-05", "window_tokens": 3000, "examples_by_attribute": {}}
{"window_id": "2023-12-05#2", "date": "2023-12-05", "window_tokens": 3000, "examples_by_attribute": {}}
{"window_id": "2023-12-05#3", "date": "2023-12-05", "window_tokens": 3000, "examples_by_attribute": {}}
{"window_id": "2023-12-05#4", "date": "2023-12-05", "window_tokens": 3000, "examples_by_attribute": {"calibration": [{"politician": "Chris Hipkins", "statement": "You understand the importance of what goes on here in a way that very few people, actually, get to understand, and I think that marks you out as being a very appropriate person for the Government to have nominated to be the Speaker of the House.", "score": 1.0, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "Stripped of hedges, the assertion he would have to defend is a judgement about who is a fit person for the Speakership \u2014 a value, not something evidence settles. He attaches it explicitly to himself ('I think'), which is exactly the right posture; conviction about a value, openly owned, is well calibrated.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "value", "confidence_markers": "'I think that marks you out as'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "Chris Hipkins", "statement": "You\u2019ve served as deputy leader of the National Party not once but twice, and you\u2019ve been a Minister in the Key Government where you held, I think, at last count, eleven different portfolios.", "score": 0.9, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "A matter of record. He states the parts he knows plainly and marks the one number he is unsure of ('I think, at last count') rather than asserting a precise figure he has not checked. Slightly below 1.0 only because the hedge is loose where the record is readily checkable.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "settled_fact", "confidence_markers": "'not once but twice' (flat); 'I think, at last count' on the portfolio count", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "David Seymour", "statement": "In a trivial sense that is true, but I can tell you about another experience of being for two terms\u2014six years\u2014the sole representative of a political party in this House, and I can tell you that the ability of any member to be able to appeal to you to uphold the rules is sometimes the only protection that this Parliament and, ultimately, our democracy has.", "score": 0.8, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The defensible assertion is an empirical claim about how minority parties are actually protected in the House \u2014 disputable, but he grounds it in named first-hand experience (six years as a sole member) and limits it with 'sometimes' rather than claiming the Standing Orders are always the only protection. 'I can tell you' is firm, but the scope qualifier keeps the posture inside what his evidence supports.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'I can tell you'; 'sometimes the only protection'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "David Seymour", "statement": "For example, we have an organisation that you will be responsible for that has a relentless push, for as long as I\u2019ve been in Parliament, and I suspect even as long as you have been, to turn MPs into corporate employees with their own HR department\u2014rules and conflict resolutions in a deadening and frankly unparliamentary way.", "score": 0.45, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The claim he would have to defend is that the Parliamentary Service has a sustained institutional agenda of a particular kind \u2014 a contested reading of an organisation's motives, asserted flatly as 'relentless'. The only hedge, 'I suspect', covers how long it has been going on, not whether it is happening; so a disputable attribution of intent is left standing as fact. Partially marked, hence mid-range.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'relentless push' (no qualification); 'I suspect' attached only to the duration", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "David Seymour", "statement": "You acknowledged, I think, how your predecessor faced it, and I can tell you, on behalf of ACT MPs and, I suspect, many more, that you will have our full support in upholding the calm, the dignity, and the traditions of this House that have been so effective in allowing New Zealanders from all walks of life to flourish better than any others have in humanity\u2019s history.", "score": 0.2, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The pledge of support is unobjectionable, but the assertion carried inside it \u2014 that this House's traditions have let New Zealanders flourish better than any institution in human history \u2014 is the one he would have to defend, and it is a sweeping comparative claim across all of history with no range, no measure and no source. The hedges present ('I think', 'I suspect') sit on trivia (what the Speaker said, how many MPs agree) while the superlative runs unqualified.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'so effective'; 'better than any others have in humanity\u2019s history' \u2014 no qualification, no source", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "Winston Peters", "statement": "Of all things to congratulate you for the post you\u2019ve taken, and knowing full well that you\u2019re qualified to do the job as Parliament\u2019s person\u2014and you\u2019re right when you say that your job is to protect the minority, in a democracy where we\u2019re one of only nine for last 100 and so years from 1854, which makes us a special, special country, and we should be preserving that to the max as we can.", "score": 0.5, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The assertion to defend is a specific count \u2014 that New Zealand is one of just nine continuous democracies since 1854 \u2014 which depends entirely on how 'democracy' and 'continuous' are defined and is genuinely disputed by people who count these things. He states the number flatly and names no source; the vague 'and so years' loosens the period but not the count. Half marks: the imprecision is in the wrong place, blurring the span while leaving the contestable figure bare.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'one of only nine' stated flatly; 'for last 100 and so years' is the only loosening; no source named", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "Winston Peters", "statement": "Strangely enough, I never thought I\u2019d be saying this, but I\u2019ve really got a lot of confidence in your ability to address people with what is a comment that is eminently fair, and when people know things are fair, they will behave themselves.", "score": 0.4, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "Two jobs in one sentence; the one he would have to defend is the general proposition that people behave when they perceive fairness \u2014 a claim about human behaviour at scale, asserted as a rule with no qualifier, no 'usually', no evidence. The first half is properly owned as his own confidence, which lifts it off the floor, but the behavioural generalisation is stated far more certainly than anyone can know it.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'I\u2019ve really got a lot of confidence' on the first half; 'they will behave themselves' \u2014 flat, no qualification", "quote_check": "verbatim"}]}}
{"window_id": "2023-12-06#0", "date": "2023-12-06", "window_tokens": 3000, "examples_by_attribute": {"calibration": [{"politician": "Chris Bishop", "statement": "I well recall when Mrs Kuriger was an inexperienced member of the Parliament, as she entered Parliament the same year as I did, in 2014, but she\u2019s now been here for nine years, entering her fourth term as the member of Parliament for Taranaki-King Country.", "score": 1.0, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "Stripped of the recollection framing this is a matter of public record \u2014 year of entry, length of service, electorate \u2014 and it is stated plainly, which is the correct posture for a checkable fact. Hedging dates and terms would be a vice, not a virtue. Nothing is forecast and no preference is dressed as a finding.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "settled_fact", "confidence_markers": "'I well recall', then flat assertion: 'she's now been here for nine years, entering her fourth term'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "Chris Bishop", "statement": "I think she enjoys the respect of the entire House for the gentle and diligent way she chairs meetings, both in committees but also in the House.", "score": 0.8, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The assertion he would have to defend is an empirical one about how 123 other members regard a colleague \u2014 not directly knowable, and he marks it as his own read with 'I think'. Points off only because the hedge sits in front of an absolute, 'the entire House', which no one can verify; the posture is otherwise honest for the kind of claim made.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'I think'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "Chris Bishop", "statement": "She\u2019s someone who I think also enjoys the confidence of the House to do an excellent job in the very important role as Assistant Speaker, so it gives me great pleasure to move her nomination.", "score": 0.85, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The defensible core is a claim about the House's view of Pugh's fitness, which is contested and unmeasured, and it is explicitly marked as the speaker's own estimate. The confidence used matches what is available to him; he does not assert that the House in fact has that confidence.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'I think', 'I think ... enjoys the confidence of the House'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "Chris Bishop", "statement": "I\u2019m sure that he will continue that diligence as the Assistant Speaker of the 54th Parliament.", "score": 0.6, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "A claim about a person's future conduct over a three-year term, asserted with 'I'm sure' and no condition or qualification. Mitigating: the certainty is explicitly located in the speaker rather than in the world, and the forecast is about one individual's habits extrapolated from an observed record, which is far more tractable than an economy-wide prediction \u2014 so this is mild overreach, not a category error.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "forecast", "confidence_markers": "'I\u2019m sure that he will'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "Chris Bishop", "statement": "Basically, what we\u2019re doing here is reinstating every item of business from the last Parliament on to the new Parliament.", "score": 0.95, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "A plain description of what a motion on the Order Paper does \u2014 checkable against the text of the motion itself, and plainly asserted, which is the right posture. He could have overclaimed about the motion's effects or necessity and did not; the only looseness is 'Basically', a summarising softener rather than a hedge on certainty.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "settled_fact", "confidence_markers": "'Basically', otherwise unqualified", "quote_check": "verbatim"}]}}
{"window_id": "2023-12-06#1", "date": "2023-12-06", "window_tokens": 3000, "examples_by_attribute": {"calibration": [{"politician": "Chris Bishop", "statement": "I think that\u2019s the appropriate constitutional thing to do. I think it\u2019s right in principle and right in a convention sense that that is what happens.", "score": 0.95, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "Strip the hedges and what remains is a judgement about what the proper constitutional course is \u2014 a value/propriety claim, which is what he would have to defend. He states it firmly but twice marks it as his own view (\"I think\"), so conviction and class fit. Firm opinion, correctly labelled as opinion.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "value", "confidence_markers": "\"I think\" (twice), \"right in principle\"", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "Prime Minister, with great power comes great responsibility, and I know that you will discharge that responsibility as a man with great courage, conviction, conscientiousness, and compassion.", "score": 0.25, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "Once the ceremonial framing is removed, the assertion he would have to defend is a forecast about how another person will behave in office over years. \"I know that you will\" claims knowledge nobody can have about future conduct; \"I trust\" or \"I believe\" would have fitted the claim. Ceremonial context explains it but the posture still outruns the claim, so not at the floor.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "forecast", "confidence_markers": "\"I know that you will\" \u2014 asserted as knowledge, no condition or qualification", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "You are selfless, you are generous, and, in my view, you will go down as one of the most important politicians the South Island will have ever seen.", "score": 0.7, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The assertion he would have to defend is the forecast about historical standing, and he does mark it as his own view, which is the right move. Partial rather than full credit because the hedge covers only the last clause while the superlative (\"one of the most important ... will have ever seen\") is a sweeping long-run judgement, and the character claims in front of it are stated flatly.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "forecast", "confidence_markers": "\"in my view\" attached to the forecast; \"you are selfless, you are generous\" unqualified", "quote_check": "verbatim"}]}}
{"window_id": "2023-12-06#2", "date": "2023-12-06", "window_tokens": 3000, "examples_by_attribute": {"calibration": [{"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "I truly believe that social investment is that change.", "score": 0.95, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "Strip the hedge and what remains is a position on what the right policy direction is \u2014 a value, not a finding. It is explicitly owned as the speaker's own conviction ('I truly believe'), so maximal conviction is correctly placed. Firm and honest at once.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "value", "confidence_markers": "'I truly believe'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "What unites us is our fundamental belief that it\u2019s the individual family unit that knows what\u2019s best for their family\u2014not the State, not the Government, and not us.", "score": 1.0, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "A philosophical commitment about where decision-making authority should sit, named as a belief held by the speaker's side rather than dressed as an empirical finding. Conviction about values, correctly labelled, is well calibrated.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "value", "confidence_markers": "'our fundamental belief'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "But, instead, we have a system which creates broken families and turns good people into lost souls.", "score": 0.2, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The assertion he would have to defend is causal: that the welfare/social system produces family breakdown. That is a genuinely disputed empirical question among competent people, and it is asserted flatly with no evidence, no source and no condition. The sweeping metaphor makes the posture harder to pin down, not softer.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "no qualification \u2014 flat present-tense assertion, 'creates', 'turns'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "The Privacy Act, with all of its good intentions, is a major barrier to getting New Zealanders the help that they need, and our approach to how we share information deserves a serious rethink.", "score": 0.3, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "Whether the Privacy Act is a major barrier to service delivery is contested \u2014 practitioners and privacy regulators disagree about whether the statute or agency practice is the constraint. Asserted as fact with no example, review or data cited. The concessive clause softens tone but qualifies nothing about the claim itself.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'is a major barrier' \u2014 no qualification; 'with all of its good intentions' concedes motive, not the claim", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "If we invest thousands in supporting the first thousand days of a child\u2019s life, we can save millions in long-term costs that stem from poor health and poor education.", "score": 0.55, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "A multi-decade fiscal forecast about early-intervention returns. It is conditionally framed and uses 'can' rather than 'will', which is real qualification, but the magnitude ('millions') arrives with no source, range or evidence base attached, so the claim still outruns its marking.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "forecast", "confidence_markers": "conditional 'If we invest...' plus modal 'we can save'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "If we are sensible with the rules and the regulations that we put in place about who can work in our education and health systems, for example, by allowing those who train in CANZUK countries\u2014Canada, Australia, New Zealand, and the United Kingdom\u2014to work here as a right, we will save millions in costs associated with burnout and the constant under-resourcing of those sectors.", "score": 0.25, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "A specific savings forecast from a labour-market registration change, asserted as a certainty. Nothing is offered on the size of the workforce response, the offsetting costs, or any modelling. The conditional is about adopting the policy, not about the uncertainty of the outcome, so it adds no hedge.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "forecast", "confidence_markers": "'we will save millions' \u2014 no range, no source, no condition beyond the policy itself", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "When we see people as having agency and dignity in their own right, rather than just as numbers on a spreadsheet, we will have a just society.", "score": 0.2, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "The thing he would have to defend is that a change in how officials regard people is sufficient for a just society \u2014 a contestable value claim delivered in the grammar of a guaranteed outcome ('when... we will'). The preference is presented as a mechanism rather than owned as a position.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "value", "confidence_markers": "'we will have a just society' \u2014 asserted as an inevitable consequence, no 'I think' or 'in my view'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "But this approach only succeeds if we are willing to follow the evidence so we can prove what works.", "score": 0.85, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "Having just forecast large savings, he attaches the condition the forecast depends on and names what would have to be shown. The posture matches a claim whose outcome is uncertain: a stated dependency rather than a guarantee.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "contested_empirical", "confidence_markers": "'only succeeds if' \u2014 the success condition is stated explicitly", "quote_check": "verbatim"}, {"politician": "James Meager", "statement": "There is no doubt in my mind that I would not be here today if it weren\u2019t for my education.", "score": 0.75, "prior_score": null, "explanation": "A counterfactual about his own life, which nobody can establish, but the certainty is explicitly located inside the speaker ('in my mind') rather than presented as a finding about the world. Marked as personal conviction, which is the right posture here; it loses a little because the counterfactual is stated as having no doubt at all.", "subject": "speaker", "subject_name": null, "falsification_criterion": null, "resolve_by": null, "proposition": null, "stance": null, "commitment": null, "responding_to": null, "claim_class": "value", "confidence_markers": "'There is no doubt in my mind'", "quote_check": "verbatim"}]}}
